---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 4: CNC Safety Certification</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Unit 2 &mdash; Manufacturing Foundations, Week 2</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 4</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">CNC Safety Certification</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>From Planning to the Machine</h2>
              <p>
                The last three sessions were about clients, materials, and
                processes.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Today, before anyone touches the router: how to use it
                without getting hurt.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Haven't We Already Done Shop Safety?</h2>
              <p>
                Yes &mdash; Unit 1 covered general shop tooling. This is
                different.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Haven't We Already Done Shop Safety?</h2>
              <p>
                A CNC router spins its bit far faster than most hand tools,
                and it <b class="text-sn-theme">moves on its own</b> once a
                job starts &mdash; you're not holding it, it's running a
                program.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Six Protocols</h2>
              <p>This is a hard gate, same as bungee safety was for the Launcher Project.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>1. Never Reach Into the Cutting Area</h2>
              <p>Not while the spindle is powered, ever &mdash; not "just to brush away chips."</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>2. Confirm the Material Is Actually Secured</h2>
              <p>
                Clamped, screwed, or held down &mdash; checked by you, not
                assumed because someone else set it up.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>3. Verify Zero/Home Before Every Job</h2>
              <p>
                Not just the first job of the day. A wrong zero point can
                send the bit straight into a clamp, the spoilboard, or the
                machine itself at full speed.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>4. Know Where the E-Stop Is</h2>
              <p>Before you press start &mdash; not while something is already going wrong.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>5. Never Leave a Running Job Unattended</h2>
              <p>A bit can break, material can shift, dust collection can fail &mdash; someone needs to be watching.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>6. Eye Protection and Dust Collection, Every Time</h2>
              <p>
                Some of our materials &mdash; MDF especially &mdash; produce
                fine dust that's a real respiratory hazard, not just a mess.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Format</h2>
              <ol>
                <li class="fragment">Instructor demonstrates the machine</li>
                <li class="fragment">You practice under direct supervision</li>
                <li class="fragment">You take a certification quiz</li>
              </ol>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Certification Quiz</h2>
              <p>Passing score: <b class="text-sn-theme">100%.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>The Certification Quiz</h2>
              <p>
                Repeatable until you pass &mdash; same standard as Unit 1's
                tool certification. Partial safety knowledge isn't safe
                knowledge.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Your Specific Machine</h2>
              <p>
                <b class="text-sn-theme">[VERIFY]</b> &mdash; the exact
                zeroing procedure, E-stop location, and startup sequence
                depend on your specific machine (ShopBot Buddy vs. Makera).
                Confirm these with your instructor before relying on them.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">This certifies you on this machine, not "CNC in general."</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A second machine with different controls means going through
                this again &mdash; same rule Unit 1 established for general
                shop tools.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">The machine doesn't know when you're not paying attention.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                It will run the program exactly as written whether or not
                anyone's watching &mdash; that's exactly why unattended
                operation is on this list.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 5: CAD Leveling-Up</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Back to the computer &mdash; building the assembly skills
                this machine will actually cut from.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Machine Demonstration</h3>
          <p>
            Watch your instructor's full demonstration of the machine before
            attempting anything yourself &mdash; startup, zeroing, running a
            job, and emergency stop.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Supervised Practice</h3>
          <p>
            Under direct supervision, practice locating the E-stop, checking
            that stock is properly secured, and verifying a zero point.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Record Your Protocol Notes</h3>
          <p>For each of the six protocols, write down one specific action you'd actually take because of it:</p>
          <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Protocol</th><th>What I'd actually do</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Never reach into the cutting area</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Confirm material is secured</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Verify zero/home before every job</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Know the E-stop location</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Never leave a running job unattended</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Eye protection &amp; dust collection</td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Certification Quiz</h3>
          <p>
            A quiz on today's protocols, administered by your instructor.
            Passing score is <b class="text-sn-theme">100%</b>, repeatable
            until you get there &mdash; this is a hard gate: no team begins
            hands-on CAM work without every member certified.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you're cleared to use the machine independently, all three must be true:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>You watched the full demonstration</li>
            <li>You practiced under direct supervision</li>
            <li>You passed the certification quiz at 100%</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Of the six protocols, which do you think would be easiest to
              get careless about after a few weeks of routine use? Why?
            </li>
            <li>
              You already hold a general shop safety certification from Unit
              1. Why does this machine need its own separate certification
              instead of just being covered by that?
            </li>
            <li>
              Now that you're certified, what's the first thing you actually
              want to design and cut on this machine?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your protocol notes and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
